00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:12.733
Let's now start the explanation.

00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:15.766
GitHub pages is a service that allows you

00:01:15.766 --> 00:01:19.532
to create a website straight from the GitHub repository.

00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:23.466
And it's also possible to create a private page

00:01:23.466 --> 00:01:26.466
that's only available with authentication,

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:30.933
but the GitHub pages does not have its own login panel.

00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:32.899
It's also on different domain.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.366
So cookies from GitHub.com are not sent there.

00:01:36.366 --> 00:01:41.132
So developers created this flow of authentication,

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:43.866
which uses cookies from GitHub.com.

00:01:43.866 --> 00:01:50.866
First, GitHub.io sets a random nonce inside GitHub pages session cookie.

00:01:51.366 --> 00:01:56.799
Then it redirects the user to GitHub.com with this nonce as the parameter,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.300
but also with a page ID and the path,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.100
then GitHub.com checks if the user is logged in,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.433
generates a token and redirects the user back to GitHub.io.

00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:12.699
Those three parameters are passed throughout the whole flow.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:17.499
Then, GitHub.io checks if the nonce from the parameter

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.100
is equal to the nonce from the cookie.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.800
If it is, the token gets inside the cookie

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.333
and the page ID is also being put inside the cookie.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.300
And with those cookies,

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.966
the user can visit GitHub page that he wanted to view.

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:39.366
In step five, the value from the parameter page ID

00:02:39.366 --> 00:02:43.199
is being put into the GitHub page’s ID cookie.

00:02:43.966 --> 00:02:46.566
Page ID is an integer number.

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:49.332
It identifies the page to which user

00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:52.433
will be redirected after the authentication flow

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.400
and the page lookup converts the cookie to integer number.

00:02:57.733 --> 00:03:02.099
So if your page ID is 123 with space at the end,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.766
the space will be dropped by integer conversion.

00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:10.066
But importantly, the cookie that will be sent back to you

00:03:10.066 --> 00:03:13.032
is the one before the int conversion.

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:14.966
So with the space at the end.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.866
You could inject %0D %0A there as it was ignored by integer conversion.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.833
Those are the CRLF characters that mean a new line.

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:32.366
Then you got a strange looking response back to your browser.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.900
Usually, CLRF vulnerability is exploited by adding new headers

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.033
or changing the response body to an XSS payload.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.333
But here you could only inject white space characters

00:03:47.333 --> 00:03:51.433
and in non white space character causes an integer conversion

00:03:51.433 --> 00:03:55.233
to throw an exception so there was no real impact to this.

00:03:55.833 --> 00:03:58.866
The researchers had access to the source code

00:03:58.866 --> 00:04:03.332
and they noticed that this page is served by nginx

00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:08.433
and nginx is written in C where null byte terminates the C-string.

00:04:08.433 --> 00:04:11.233
So it's always worth to give it a try.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.966
Turned out it worked,

00:04:13.966 --> 00:04:18.499
you could inject any characters after the null byte and they were ignored,

00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:24.266
but null byte is not allowed in the header section of the response.

00:04:24.566 --> 00:04:27.599
So you could only inject something in the body.

00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:31.533
In fact, this was a 302 redirect,

00:04:31.533 --> 00:04:35.699
where generally the response body is not processed by the browser.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.966
But here, luckily the location header was after our injection point,

00:04:40.966 --> 00:04:43.399
so it was not treated as header.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.000
And in 302 responses, without the location header,

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.133
the body is processed normally.

00:04:50.066 --> 00:04:53.699
With that, we can inject the HTML code in the response

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.433
and execute arbitrary JavaScript code, reaching our XSS.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.366
But the problem is that this injection is in fifth request of the whole flow.

00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:08.999
And you can't just take this one, put whatever in other parameters

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.133
and it will work. No, it wouldn't.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:15.166
You need to send the victim into a valid authentication flow

00:05:15.166 --> 00:05:17.799
 just with this one malicious parameter.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:24.333
And as you remember, we had three parameters there - nonce, path, and page ID.

00:05:24.333 --> 00:05:29.099
Page ID will be our payload, path doesn't matter much.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.333
The problem is the nonce.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.700
Because GitHub.io checks if the nonce from the parameter

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:41.366
that comes from GitHub.com is the same as nonce in the cookie.

00:05:42.066 --> 00:05:46.866
So you either need to predict the nonce or to set the cookie

00:05:46.866 --> 00:05:51.766
to your known value and then send the same value as the parameter.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.733
The potential attack scenario is that we have right access

00:05:56.733 --> 00:06:00.233
to attacker.private-org.github.io

00:06:00.233 --> 00:06:04.533
and we want to read another page from the same organization.

00:06:04.533 --> 00:06:06.633
Let's say it's called flag.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.266
Such scenario with user interaction

00:06:10.266 --> 00:06:15.499
and being in the same organization would be granted $5,000 of bonus.

00:06:16.466 --> 00:06:21.266
Our bug hunters decided to try the scenario where you set the cookie first,

00:06:21.266 --> 00:06:27.232
considering we can’t control other sub domain of private-org.github.io.

00:06:27.233 --> 00:06:29.233
This should be a bit easier.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.900
The problem was that the cookie within nonce used the __Host- prefix.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.600
It means that from attacker subdomain, you can only set the cookie

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:43.300
to attacker subdomain and not to the parent domain.

00:06:43.933 --> 00:06:47.299
If there wasn't this prefix from attacker.private-org.github.io,

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.733
you could just set the cookie to the parent subdomain

00:06:50.733 --> 00:06:54.533
and then it would also be sent to flag.private-org.github.io,

00:06:54.533 --> 00:06:57.666
meaning that, you know the nonce from the subdomain,

00:06:57.666 --> 00:07:00.266
and you can execute the cross-site scripting.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.533
So you need to trick the server into thinking that the cookie has the __Host- prefix

00:07:06.533 --> 00:07:09.133
while the browser thinks it doesn't.

00:07:09.833 --> 00:07:14.066
Turns out that the __Host- prefix is case sensitive.

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:20.099
This means if you try to set the __Host- cookie to parent domain, it won't work.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:24.800
But if you try the same with __HOST- using capital letters, it will work

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.100
because the browser doesn't consider this to be a valid host prefix,

00:07:29.666 --> 00:07:34.866
but turns out that the server used by GitHub doesn't care about it at all.

00:07:35.466 --> 00:07:40.732
While parsing cookies, it will strip the host part whatever the capitalization.

00:07:41.533 --> 00:07:44.933
So you can set the host cookie using capital letters.

00:07:44.933 --> 00:07:47.966
The browser will set it to the parent domain

00:07:47.966 --> 00:07:52.366
because it's not a valid prefix while the server will also be happy

00:07:52.366 --> 00:07:56.132
because it thinks that the cookie has the host prefix.

00:07:56.666 --> 00:07:59.799
So this is the proof of concept that will be hosted

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:03.433
on attacker.private-org.github.io.

00:08:03.966 --> 00:08:08.932
It will send the cookie to parent domain with nonce known by us.

00:08:08.933 --> 00:08:15.299
And will send the victim into a valid authentication flow with nonce known by us.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.133
And with page ID of the page that we want to attack.

00:08:19.133 --> 00:08:21.466
Once the victim goes through the flow,

00:08:21.466 --> 00:08:26.032
it gets back to GitHub page where the XSS is executed.

00:08:26.733 --> 00:08:29.766
This way, you could access any page

00:08:29.766 --> 00:08:32.699
that the attack victim would have access to.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.666
This made hunters eligible for $5,000 bonus,

00:08:37.533 --> 00:08:39.333
but they were hungry for more.

00:08:39.333 --> 00:08:43.166
They wanted to escalate it to a no interaction bug.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.800
And this escalation was made rather easy by cache poisoning vulnerability.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.333
It was present on the same vulnerable endpoint

00:08:53.333 --> 00:08:58.799
and the page was cached based on the integer value of page ID.

00:08:59.333 --> 00:09:04.699
So if you would request the page with ID "123", or "123 ",

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.366
you would get the same cached response.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:11.800
So if you would request it with our payload used previously,

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.333
then everyone that will visit the same page would get XSSed by you.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:21.200
This means you'd no longer need user's interaction.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:26.533
And you attack all pages of victims that visit this malicious page.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.800
So this attack scenario no longer needed user interaction

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.500
and only required a read access to a page.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:38.933
Such scenario would be granted $10,000 of bonus.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.933
Now to get the maximum $15,000 bonus,

00:09:42.933 --> 00:09:48.666
they needed to escalate it to an attacker that is outside the organization.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.966
So if a GitHub page is completely public,

00:09:53.966 --> 00:09:57.132
then this whole authentication flow doesn't happen.

00:09:57.833 --> 00:10:03.466
But in case that there is a private repository that is then changed to public,

00:10:03.466 --> 00:10:08.399
then each user that visits the page, still goes through the authentication flow,

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.966
but every GitHub user has read access to this private page.

00:10:14.466 --> 00:10:17.999
And as you already saw read access to a page

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.700
is everything that you need to poison the cache with the XSS payload.

00:10:23.566 --> 00:10:27.099
This means that the attack can be executed

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:30.200
by the attacker outside the organization.

00:10:30.766 --> 00:10:34.099
All you need is a repository that was private

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:38.100
and had a GitHub private page, but was then changed to public.

00:10:39.266 --> 00:10:44.932
And with that attack, our hunters were rewarded $15,000 of maximum bonus

00:10:44.933 --> 00:10:50.966
plus $20,000 of base payment for a total of $35,000.

00:10:52.233 --> 00:10:57.399
That's very good money, especially when you are 14 or 17 years old.

00:10:57.933 --> 00:10:58.933
And that's it.

00:10:59.233 --> 00:11:03.133
It's amazing how far little bugs were changed together

00:11:03.133 --> 00:11:06.266
for a vulnerability with such a huge impact.

